<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Recognizing Related Equations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00157</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Recognizing Related Equations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>e78f4665-a498-4b7b-9760-5512236d2895</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00001"><title>Cool Down</title>
      <para id="para-00001">A cardboard box, which weighs 0.6 pound when empty, is filled with 15 bags of beans and a 4-pound bag of rice. The total weight of the box and the contents inside it is 25.6 pounds. One way to represent this situation is with the equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>0.6</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>15</m:mn><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>25.6</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00002">In this situation, what does the solution to the equation represent?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">The solution is the weight of a bag of beans.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="e95f1302-e095-488f-b28c-277cdb16ef09" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00004">Identify the <emphasis>three</emphasis> equations that are equivalent to 0.6+15b+4=25.6.</para>
          <list id="list-00001">
            <item><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
              <mn>15</mn>
              <mi>b</mi>
              <mo>+</mo>
              <mn>4</mn>
              <mo>=</mo>
              <mn>25.6</mn>
            </math></item>
            <item xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
              <mn>15</mn>
              <mi>b</mi>
              <mo>+</mo>
              <mn>4</mn>
              <mo>=</mo>
              <mn>25</mn>
            </math></item>
            <item xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
              <mn>3</mn>
              <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
              <mn>0.6</mn>
              <mo>+</mo>
              <mn>15</mn>
              <mi>b</mi>
              <mo>+</mo>
              <mn>4</mn>
              <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
              <mo>=</mo>
              <mn>76.8</mn>
            </math></item>
            <item xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
              <mn>15</mn>
              <mi>b</mi>
              <mo>=</mo>
              <mn>25.6</mn>
            </math></item>
            <item xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
              <mn>15</mn>
              <mi>b</mi>
              <mo>=</mo>
              <mn>21</mn>
            </math></item>
          </list>
        </problem>
        <solution xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00005"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>15</m:mn><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>25</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0.6</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>15</m:mn><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>76.8</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>15</m:mn><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>21</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. Correctly applying the properties of real numbers and using inverse operations helps you recognize equivalent equations.</para>
          
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>